export const lpContent={
'head':{
 title:'Head',one:'The round top of the whole body — home of the face, the brain and every nod and shake your child owns.',
 seoTitle:'Head Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what the head is for, the nod-and-shake games, the sense organs it carries and why it sits on top at all.',
 intro:'This lesson starts at the very top. The head is the body part your child has known longest — it shook no before any words arrived — and naming it properly opens the whole map of the face and the five senses living on it.',
 what:'The head is the top part of the body: a round home for the face, the brain inside and all the sense organs — eyes, ears, nose and mouth. Everything your child uses to see, hear, smell, taste and talk is gathered in this one place.',
 mean:'The head sits on top for a reason: it is the body’s lookout tower. High up, the eyes see further, the ears catch more, and the brain inside gets first news of everything. Your child’s head also does the showing — nodding yes, shaking no, tilting to listen — before any words are needed.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: the head is the body part on top, it holds the face and the brain, hats and hoods and hairbrushes all live on it, and it moves in useful ways — nodding for yes, shaking for no, tilting when something is interesting.',
 why:'Body-part naming is the first map a child ever learns, and the head is its capital. A child who can name the head and everything on it can follow directions, describe aches and feelings, and take part in the games — heads up, head down — that preschool runs all day.',
 dev:'Body-part vocabulary and body schema (the internal map of self), imitation and motor control in the nod-and-shake games, the five-sense organs as a set, and self-identity — my head, my face, my smile.',
 vocab:'head, face, brain, nod, shake, top',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what body part this is — the smiling boy’s face with dark hair makes the answer easy. Then point to the word: head.\n2) Find your child’s head with both hands: round on top, face at the front, hair up top. Name each neighbour — eyes, ears, nose, mouth — as you touch near it.\n3) Play the head-moves game: nod your head yes, shake your head no, tilt it slowly side to side like you are listening. Your child copies each one.\n4) Name what lives on the head: a hat goes here, a hood goes here, the hairbrush visits every morning. Head as a place, not just a part.\n5) Finish with the lookout tower idea: the head sits on top so the eyes can see far and the ears can hear wide. Ask your child to look left, look right, from their tower.',
 say:'"This is your head — the very top of you! Your head holds your face, your eyes, your ears, your nose and your mouth, and your brain works inside it all day. Nod your head: yes! Shake your head: no! Your head talks before your mouth does."',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: whose face is this — a smiling boy’s? What color is his hair? Which word sits under his face? What is your own head doing right now?',
 ask:'"What is the top part of your body called?" "What does your head carry — name them all!" "How does your head say yes? And no?" "What do we put on our heads when it is cold?"',
 childSay:'Your child may nod and shake through every question for a day, name the whole face-country map — eyes here, ears here, nose here — or report proudly that the brain lives inside and it is thinking right now.',
 real:'Heads are named all day long: heads up at crossing the street, brush your hair, hat on for the cold, chin up at the doctor’s. Every one of those daily phrases lands better on a child who knows exactly which part the word means.',
 play:'Follow-my-head: you move only your head — nod, shake, tilt, look up, peek down — and your child copies like a mirror. Swap roles. Add slow-motion mode and robot mode. It is imitation, motor control and connection in one seated game.',
 check:'Ask: "What is this body part? What does your head carry? Show me yes and no with your head." The name, the list and the nod — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Touch is what makes body maps stick — name each part while your hand is on it, not from across the room. And let the head be silly sometimes: a head that can tilt like a curious bird or droop like a sleepy puppy is a head fully explored.'},
'eyes':{
 title:'Eyes',one:'Two brown eyes, always working in a team — the seeing pair that finds lost socks and watches for butterflies.',
 seoTitle:'Eyes Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what eyes do, why they blink, the staring and blinking games and the honest rules for protecting sight.',
 intro:'This lesson meets the busiest pair on the whole body. Your child’s eyes have been working since before birth — watching, finding, reading the world — and now they get their name, their games and their care rules.',
 what:'Eyes are the two seeing parts of the body, sitting side by side in the face. They take in light and turn it into pictures inside your head — the dog in the garden, the page in the book, the face across the table. They work in a team: both eyes look at the same thing at once.',
 mean:'Seeing feels effortless, which is exactly why it hides its own magic: light comes in, and pictures get made. The eyes also guard themselves — blinking spreads a tiny wash of tears that keeps them clean and comfortable, hundreds of times a day without anyone deciding to.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: eyes are for seeing, they come as a pair and work together, they blink all by themselves to stay wet and clean, and they are precious — no poking, no rubbing when hands are dirty, and no looking straight at the sun, ever.',
 why:'The eyes carry most of the information a preschooler learns from — books, faces, puzzles, the world. A child who knows their eyes by name can also know their needs: it is too bright, I need to blink, my eyes are tired — the first steps of self-care for sight.',
 dev:'Body-part vocabulary, the seeing sense as a named ability, teamwork awareness (two eyes, one picture), self-protection habits, and observation games that train focus and visual attention.',
 vocab:'eyes, see, blink, look, pair, bright',
 teach:'1) Show the card and count the eyes together: one, two — a pair. Ask what they do, then name it: they see.\n2) Find your child’s eyes in a mirror: point to each one, then blink together. The blink is automatic — point out that nobody decides to do it.\n3) Play the staring contest: who can keep both eyes open the longest without giggling? Then flip it — who can blink the fastest, five blinks in a row?\n4) Prove the teamwork: hold one finger in front of your child’s nose and watch both eyes swing to meet it. Two eyes, one job.\n5) Set the care rules gently: eyes get clean hands around them, no poking, and never a look at the sun. Eyes are precious, and precious things get care.',
 say:'"These are your eyes — one, two, a pair! They see the dog, the book, my face, everything. And they blink all by themselves, wash, blink, all day long, to keep themselves clean. Eyes are precious: gentle hands, no rubbing, and never looking at the sun. Deal?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: how many eyes are in the picture? What color are they? What word sits above them? Are they open or closed?',
 ask:'"What do your eyes do?" "Why do we blink — who decided that?" "What did your eyes see first thing this morning?" "What do we never look at, even for a second?"',
 childSay:'Your child may announce blink counts at breakfast, catch their own reflection and name both eyes, or warn a teddy solemnly about the sun — the seeing pair, named and defended.',
 real:'Eyes work everywhere: spotting the bus, finding the missing shoe, following a ladybug across a leaf, choosing the reddest apple. Name what the eyes found — "your eyes spotted it!" — and the pair gets credit for a full day of work.',
 play:'Eye-spy points: take turns being the caller — "something red!" "something round!" "something high up!" — and the finder must point with their eyes before their finger. It is visual search practice, colour review and turn-taking in one fast game.',
 check:'Ask: "What do your eyes do? How many are there? Why do they blink?" The seeing, the pair and the self-washing answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Do the finger-to-nose teamwork demo once with your own eyes too — feeling your own eyes swing is surprisingly delightful for adults. And treat the sun rule as absolute, not negotiable: eyes and sunshine meet through hats, shade and sunglasses only.',
 safety:'Eyes are delicate: dirty hands stay away from them, no poking or prodding, and looking straight at the sun — even briefly, even through clouds — can damage sight for life. Bright days get hats and shade; anything that lands in an eye gets a grown-up and clean water, fast.'},
'ears':{
 title:'Ears',one:'One on each side, never off duty — the hearing pair that catches dogs, doorbells and songs all day long.',
 seoTitle:'Ears Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what ears do, the cover-and-listen game, sounds versus quiet and the honest rules for protecting little ears.',
 intro:'This lesson tunes in to the hearing pair. Ears never sleep, never blink and never switch off — they catch the world all night and all day — and naming them opens up a whole set of listening games your house is already full of.',
 what:'Ears are the two hearing parts of the body, one on each side of the head. They catch sounds — voices, dogs, doorbells, songs, thunder — and pass them to the brain, which works out what each one is. Even asleep, ears stay on duty.',
 mean:'Hearing is the one sense that never closes: eyes shut for sleep, but ears keep listening, which is why a familiar voice can wake a child and a lullaby can settle one. Sound arrives from every direction — front, back, through walls — making ears the body’s surround-sound system.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: ears are for hearing, they come as a pair on the sides of the head, they work even when eyes are shut, and they are delicate — nothing ever goes inside an ear, and very loud noise right up close is too much for them.',
 why:'Listening is half of every conversation and all of every song, story and instruction. A child who knows their ears can also train them: the quiet games in this lesson build the attention that circle time, music class and safety — hearing a warning call — all depend on.',
 dev:'Auditory attention and discrimination (telling sounds apart), body-part vocabulary, volume awareness, the quiet-stillness skill that group learning needs, and the sensory delight of a world heard with intent.',
 vocab:'ears, hear, listen, sound, loud, quiet',
 teach:'1) Show the card and point: what are these, and what are they for? Name them — ears — and count: one on each side.\n2) Find your child’s ears: trace the curve, tug gently, then wiggle your own and admit how hard it is. Some people can, most cannot — trying is the fun.\n3) Play cover-and-listen: cup your hands over your ears and hear the world go quiet — then let go and name every sound that rushes back in.\n4) Prove the all-directions trick: your child shuts their eyes while you hide and call their name — the ears find you without any looking at all.\n5) Set the care rules: nothing goes in an ear, not even a finger, and ears need a break from very loud noise right up close. Ears are on duty for life.',
 say:'"These are your ears — one here, one here, a pair! They catch everything: my voice, the dog next door, the kettle, the song on the radio. They even listen while you sleep! And they are delicate — nothing ever goes inside an ear. Ears are for listening, from outside only."',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: how many ears are in the picture? Where do they sit — on the sides? What word sits under them? Can you see a hole or a curve in each one?',
 ask:'"What do your ears do?" "What was the first sound you heard this morning?" "Do ears sleep when you sleep?" "What do we never put inside our ears?"',
 childSay:'Your child may narrate the household soundscape — fridge humming, door creaking, daddy coughing — point out the ears of every animal they meet, or cup their hands behind their own ears and announce that the world got louder.',
 real:'Ears earn their keep everywhere: hearing the doorbell, the timer, the bath running, a sibling crying two rooms away, a warning shout at the road. Name the ears’ catches as they happen — "your ears heard that first!" — and the pair becomes a celebrated instrument.',
 play:'The sound detective: your child closes their eyes while you make a mystery sound — keys, a spoon in a cup, a page turning, a zipper — and they name it. Six sounds, six guesses, endless replays. It is listening, memory and deduction with objects from every drawer.',
 check:'Ask: "What do your ears do? How many and where? What do we never put in them?" The hearing, the pair and the nothing-inside rule — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'The hide-and-call game doubles as separation reassurance — ears prove that a hidden parent is still findable. And keep music and screens at conversation volume as the household default: little ears do their best growing at that level, and the rule is easiest when it is everyone’s.',
 safety:'Nothing ever goes inside an ear — not fingers, not cotton buds, not toys; ears clean themselves, and anything stuck gets a doctor, not a dig. Loud sound close to the ear — shouts, speakers, headphones at full — can harm hearing, so keep volume at talking level and step back from the very loud.'},
'nose':{
 title:'Nose',one:'The sniffer in the middle of the face — smell machine, breathing door and the star of every sniffing game.',
 seoTitle:'Nose Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what the nose does, the sniff-and-guess game, breathing through the nose and the nothing-up-the-nose rule.',
 intro:'This lesson puts the sniffer centre stage. The nose does three jobs at once — smelling, breathing, and being very good at finding bananas — and the sniffing games that teach it are the guaranteed-giggle kind your child will demand again tomorrow.',
 what:'The nose is the smelling part of the body, sitting in the middle of the face. It breathes air in and out through two little doorways, and it catches smells riding on that air — dinner cooking, flowers, rain, socks — and reports them all to the brain.',
 mean:'Smell is the sneakiest sense: it works without being asked, and it is the fastest road to memory there is. One sniff of a familiar smell can bring back a whole place — grandma’s kitchen, the bakery, the swimming pool. The nose is a reporter sending invisible news upstairs.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: the nose smells and breathes, sniffing is how it pulls smells up to be read, some smells are lovely and some are warnings — smoke, gone-off food — and nothing ever goes inside a nose, not fingers, not beans, not anything.',
 why:'A named nose becomes a working nose. Children who can say what they smell join a whole layer of the world most people ignore — and smell is also a safety sense: burnt toast, smoke, spoiled milk all announce themselves to a nose that is listening.',
 dev:'Olfactory vocabulary and attention, breathing awareness (nose in, mouth out — the runner’s trick), the sniff-and-guess memory game, safety associations with warning smells, and the giggle engine of any good sniffing contest.',
 vocab:'nose, smell, sniff, breathe, stinky, fresh',
 teach:'1) Show the card and find the little nose: whose is it, and what is it for? Name it, then sniff the air dramatically to prove the point.\n2) Find your child’s nose in a mirror: touch it, wiggle it, breathe in and out through it and feel the air moving.\n3) Play the sniff test: three things with smells — a piece of fruit, a clean sock, a spoonful of something from the spice shelf — sniffed with eyes shut. Guess before you look.\n4) Teach the runner’s trick: breathe in through the nose, out through the mouth — in through the nose, out through the mouth. It is how noses and mouths share the work.\n5) Set the rule with a straight face: nothing goes in a nose. Not a finger, not a pea, not a bead. Noses are doorways, not cupboards.',
 say:'"This is your nose — right in the middle, the best seat in the house! It breathes in and out all day and it smells everything: bananas, flowers, rain, dinner. Sniff, sniff — see? Sniffing pulls the smell up so your brain can read it. And nothing ever goes inside a nose. Noses sniff, that is all!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: where does the little nose sit — in the middle of the face? What word is written beside it? Can you find your own nose without using your hands — by wiggling it?',
 ask:'"What does your nose do — all its jobs?" "What is the best smell in our house? The worst?" "How does sniffing help?" "What do we never put in our nose?"',
 childSay:'Your child may sniff the air like a bloodhound at every meal, report dinner from the next room before it is announced, or patrol the house for stinky smells with the seriousness of an inspector — the nose, fully on duty.',
 real:'Noses work everywhere: the bread aisle at the shop, the rain on the walk home, the rubbish bin that needs taking out, the baby sibling’s head. Each smell is a sentence the nose writes — name a few, and the world gains a whole quiet channel.',
 play:'Sniff-and-guess kitchen: set out three covered cups, each hiding a smell — orange peel, a mint leaf, a dab of toothpaste. Sniff with eyes shut, guess, uncover, cheer. Rotate the cast daily; the nose gets sharper with practice, and the guessing never gets old.',
 check:'Ask: "What does your nose do? What does sniffing do? What goes in a nose?" The two jobs, the pulling-up answer and the loud nothing — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the sniff test to food and kitchen smells — the safe category — and let your child add one mystery smell per round. If the guessing stalls, sniff together and compare notes out loud: two noses report differently, and comparing is the fun.',
 safety:'Nothing goes inside a nose — beads, beans, fingers and paper all end up in emergencies, and a nose is a doorway, not a drawer. Sniffing games use food and flowers only: cleaning products, spices up close and anything from a bottle under the sink stay off the list, and strong smells get sniffed from a distance.'},
'mouth':{
 title:'Mouth',one:'The multi-tool of the face — eating, talking, smiling and humming, all from one open-and-shut doorway.',
 seoTitle:'Mouth Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: the many jobs of the mouth, the mouth-shapes game, teeth and tongue as workmates and what belongs in a mouth.',
 intro:'This lesson opens the busiest doorway on the face. The mouth takes in dinner, sends out words, shows the whole world a smile and hums along to every song — and your child has been running it, uncredited, since day one.',
 what:'The mouth is the opening below the nose, framed by lips. Food and drink go in, words and laughs and songs come out, and inside it works a team: teeth for chewing, tongue for tasting and moving, and a smile showing to everyone.',
 mean:'The mouth is the body’s front door for two of its biggest jobs: fuel and language. Every word your child says travels out of this one doorway, shaped by lips and tongue; every bite that becomes energy travels in through it. It is also the sense-organ neighbour where taste happens — the tongue lives here.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: the mouth eats, drinks, talks, smiles and hums, the lips frame it and the teeth and tongue work inside it, it opens wide for the dentist and says aaah — and only food and drink belong inside it, nothing else, not ever.',
 why:'Naming the mouth gives your child a word for the place where eating meets talking meets feelings — the spot that hurts at the dentist, tingles at the toothbrush and aches when a wobbly tooth arrives. Body maps with the mouth on them handle all of those moments better.',
 dev:'Body-part vocabulary, oral-motor awareness (mouth shapes as controls), the food-and-words double duty, self-care links to tooth-brushing, and the safety rule that keeps non-food objects out.',
 vocab:'mouth, lips, teeth, tongue, smile, chew',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what body part is peeking out from behind this big open smile? Let the grin give it away, then name its jobs: it eats, it talks, it smiles.\n2) Make a mouth-shape show together: small ooh mouth, wide aah mouth, big smile, tiny hmm. Ask which shape the card is showing.\n3) Name the workmates: lips on the outside, teeth for chewing, tongue for tasting and moving food. Say aaah and find all three in a mirror.\n4) Talk through the door: put a hand gently on your lips while saying a word and feel the buzz. Every word your child says comes out of this doorway.\n5) Set the membership rule: mouths admit food, drink, words and toothbrushes — nothing else. Pebbles, crayons and toy parts stay out; the mouth has a guest list.',
 say:'"This is your mouth — what a busy doorway! Food goes in, words come out, smiles show and songs hum. Inside: teeth chewing, tongue tasting, lips framing it all. Open wide — aaah! And remember the guest list: food and drink only. Everything else stays out of the mouth."',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: is the mouth open or closed? Can you see a smile? What word sits beside it? Are any teeth showing?',
 ask:'"What are all the jobs your mouth does?" "What is inside your mouth that chews? That tastes?" "What shape is your mouth making right now?" "What belongs in a mouth — and what never does?"',
 childSay:'Your child may perform the whole mouth-shape alphabet on request, report that the tongue lives inside and the teeth are its neighbours, or guard a pebble from a younger sibling with the guest-list rule quoted word for word.',
 real:'Mouths work everywhere: chewing dinner, blowing bubbles, whistling at the kettle, saying sorry, singing in the car. The dentist visit is the field trip — the wide-open aaah is exactly the mouth shape this lesson practised.',
 play:'Mouth-shape charades: you make a mouth shape — ooh, aah, wide smile, small circle — and your child guesses what goes with it: blowing a candle, saying aaah at the doctor, smiling at a friend, drinking through a straw. Swap roles. Every round is oral-motor practice disguised as a guessing game.',
 check:'Ask: "Name three jobs your mouth does. What chews inside it? What goes in a mouth?" The list, the teeth and the food-only rule — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Connect the mouth lesson to tooth-brushing night — same doorway, same teeth, same tongue — and the routine gains a body-map reason. If mouthing of objects still happens at three, the guest-list rule said cheerfully, and often, does more than any confiscation.',
 safety:'Only food and drink go in the mouth — pebbles, batteries, button coins and toy parts are mouth hazards, not snacks, and small objects stay away from little siblings. Biting is for food: friends, pets and furniture are all outside the guest list.'},
'hands':{
 title:'Hands',one:'Two open hands in striped sleeves — the grab, clap, build and help machines your child already runs all day.',
 seoTitle:'Hands Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: the many jobs of hands, clap-fast-clap-slow games, left and right, washing as hand care and helping hands.',
 intro:'This lesson gives names to the busiest workers on the body. Hands grab, clap, wave, build, draw and help — they are how your child touches the whole world — and they come with their own care routine your child already half knows.',
 what:'Hands are the two grabbing parts at the ends of the arms, each with a palm and five fingers. They hold spoons and crayons, clap and wave, build towers, turn pages, stroke pets and help carry — nearly everything your child does with the world goes through the hands.',
 mean:'Hands are the body’s main tool users — and its main touchers. Through the hands, your child learns what things feel like: smooth, bumpy, warm, soft. And hands are social too: they clap for friends, wave hello, point at wonders and reach out for comfort before any words get there.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: hands hold, clap, wave and help, each hand has five fingers, they come as a left and a right, and they need care — washed before eating and after the toilet, dried, and kept gentle with friends and pets.',
 why:'Hand skills are the school-readiness skills: holding crayons, turning pages, managing zips and buttons. Naming the hands and celebrating their work builds both vocabulary and pride — and the washing link turns a body-part lesson into a hygiene habit with a reason attached.',
 dev:'Fine-motor vocabulary and pride, hand preference as it emerges (left or right), touch learning through the palms, hygiene habits with meaning, and the social language of clapping, waving, pointing and helping.',
 vocab:'hands, fingers, clap, wave, hold, wash',
 teach:'1) Show the card: two open hands in striped sleeves, fingers spread. Count the fingers on the card, then on your child’s real hand — five each.\n2) Name the jobs: clap, wave, hold, point, help. Do each one together — a clap, a wave, a spoon held up, a point at the window, a toy handed over.\n3) Play clap-fast-clap-slow: speed the claps up, slow them down, clap high, clap low, then hide both hands behind your back — where did they go?\n4) Introduce left and right: hold a hand up, thumb out — the L-shape is the left. Try both ways and see which one makes the letter.\n5) Finish with hand care: pretend to wash — palms, backs, between the fingers — and name the moments: before eating, after the toilet, after playing outside.',
 say:'"These are your hands — two busy workers! They clap, they wave, they hold your spoon and build your towers. Five fingers each, a left and a right. And hands have rules: washed before we eat, gentle with our friends, and always helping. Show me your helping hands!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: how many hands are in the picture? Are the fingers open or closed? What do the sleeves look like — stripes? What word sits between the hands?',
 ask:'"What can your hands do — name five jobs!" "How many fingers does each hand have?" "Which hand is your left one?" "When do hands need washing?"',
 childSay:'Your child may count all ten fingers twice a day, announce which hand is the drawing hand, or offer a helping hand at every chore with the phrase attached — the workers, named and proud.',
 real:'Hands are named all day: wash your hands, hold my hand at the road, clap for the birthday child, gentle hands with the cat. Each daily phrase is a repetition — and each one lands better on a child who knows exactly what a hand is.',
 play:'Helping-hands mission: give your child three real jobs that only hands can do — carry the spoons to the table, fold the tea towel, post the letter in the drawer. Narrate each as hand-work: your hands carried it, your hands folded it, your hands posted it. Pride follows the naming.',
 check:'Ask: "What do hands do? How many fingers? When do we wash them?" The job list, the five and the before-eating answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Follow your child’s lead on left versus right — preference settles by itself around this age, and forcing it slows it down. The L-trick is enough. And let helping hands be a real job title in the family: children work brilliantly for a title they hold.',
 safety:'Hands are for gentle: hitting, grabbing and throwing-at-friends are outside their job description, and the rule is easiest to teach as what hands are for, not just what they are not. Wash before eating and after the toilet — the palm-and-back-and-between-fingers way.'},
'feet':{
 title:'Feet',one:'Two bare feet, ready for anything — the stomp, tiptoe and jump machines that carry your child everywhere.',
 seoTitle:'Feet Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what feet do, stomp-and-freeze games, tiptoe control, socks and shoes as feet care and counting the ten toes.',
 intro:'This lesson gets down to the ground floor. Feet carry your child across every day of their life — and they are the bounciest body part to name, because foot games are standing games, and standing games are loud ones.',
 what:'Feet are the two walking parts at the bottom of the legs, each with a sole, a heel and five toes. They stand, walk, run, jump, kick, pedal and balance — every trip your child takes, the feet do the travelling.',
 mean:'Feet are the body’s transport and its balance system: they tell the brain what the ground is like — soft rug, hard tiles, squishy grass — and adjust every step. That reporting is why barefoot play matters: feet learn the world best with nothing between them and it.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: feet walk, run, jump and balance, ten toes live on them — five each — they report the ground to the brain, and they get care: socks when cold, shoes for outside, washed at bath time after a barefoot day.',
 why:'Foot vocabulary powers the movement words preschool runs on — stand, jump, kick, tiptoe, stomp — and the games that teach it build real gross-motor control. A child who can go from giant stomp to mouse tiptoe on command has balance, listening and impulse control all in one game.',
 dev:'Gross-motor control (stomping, tiptoeing, freezing), balance and body awareness, foot vocabulary, counting to ten on toes, and self-care habits — socks, shoes, washing — with reasons attached.',
 vocab:'feet, toes, stomp, tiptoe, jump, balance',
 teach:'1) Show the card: two bare feet, toes included. Count the toes on the card, then on your child’s real feet — five per foot, ten altogether.\n2) Name the jobs: stand, walk, run, jump, kick, tiptoe. Try each one on the spot — a jump, a kick in the air, a wobbly balance on one foot.\n3) Play stomp-and-tiptoe: stomp like a giant — loud, heavy, shaking the floor — then switch to tiptoe like a mouse, small and silent. Alternate on your call.\n4) Add the freeze: after any stomp or tiptoe round, call freeze! — both feet still, balancing. Freezing is the hard part and the funny one.\n5) Finish with feet care: socks when the floor is cold, shoes for outside, washed at bath time. Feet work all day; they get looked after at night.',
 say:'"These are your feet — the walkers, the jumpers, the runners! Ten toes on board, five on each. They can stomp like giants — STOMP STOMP — or sneak like mice on tiptoe, not a sound. And after all that walking, feet get washed, socks and shoes included. Feet are the bosses of going places."',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: are these bare feet or do they have shoes on? How many toes can you count? What word sits above them? Are the feet side by side or one in front?',
 ask:'"What do your feet do all day?" "How many toes — on each foot, and altogether?" "Show me a giant stomp. Now a mouse tiptoe." "What do feet wear outside?"',
 childSay:'Your child may stomp-tiptoe-freeze through the hallway on request, count all ten toes at bath time, or demand barefoot time as a grass-reporting mission — the transport department, fully staffed and proud.',
 real:'Feet mark every journey: the walk to the park, the pedal on the trike, the kick of the ball, the wobble across a stepping stone. Name the work — your feet climbed it, your feet pedalled it — and the bottom of the body gets the credit it earns.',
 play:'Stomp-and-freeze parade: music on, everyone marches; the music stops, you call a foot command — freeze, tiptoe, jump, stand on one foot — and everyone obeys until the next round. The switching is the training: big moves, small moves, stillness, all on one word.',
 check:'Ask: "What do feet do? How many toes? Show me tiptoe — now freeze." The list, the ten and a wobbly balanced freeze — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Give barefoot minutes on safe floors and real grass: the ground-reporting is the point, and children love naming what their soles found. And keep the stomp games off tiles with sleeping babies — feet lessons are best held on rugs and lawns.',
 safety:'Feet meet the world first: watch for hot pavement in summer, sharp things on lawns and slippery socks on wooden stairs — grip socks or bare feet for climbing, shoes for outside, and a grown-up for anything that stings.'},
'arms':{
 title:'Arms',one:'Two reaching, hugging, lifting arms — the long levers your child uses to hold, swing and wrap the world closer.',
 seoTitle:'Arms Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what arms do, the tree-and-airplane shapes, hugging and lifting games and how sleeves make arms practice.',
 intro:'This lesson reaches out. Arms are the long levers between shoulders and hands — they lift, swing, wrap and carry — and they are the body part your child uses most for the things that matter most: holding on and hugging.',
 what:'Arms are the two long parts between the shoulders and the hands. They reach up high, stretch out wide, lift and carry, swing when walking, and wrap around for hugs. At the end of every arm is a hand — the arms deliver it wherever it needs to go.',
 mean:'Arms are the delivery system for the hands: everything hands do, arms reach for first. They are also the hug machines — the wrap-around part of every cuddle — and the balancers, flung out wide whenever a beam or a curb needs crossing.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: arms reach, lift, carry, swing and hug, they connect the shoulders to the hands, they stretch in three useful shapes — up like a tree, out like an airplane, around like a hug — and sleeves are the tunnels arms travel through when dressing.',
 why:'Arm strength and control power the whole preschool day: climbing, carrying chairs, painting big, catching balls. Naming the arms and playing their shapes builds the vocabulary of movement — reach, lift, stretch, wrap — that PE lessons and playgrounds run on.',
 dev:'Gross-motor reach, lift and carry strength, spatial language (up, out, around), dressing independence through sleeve practice, and the emotional work of hugging — arms as the instruments of comfort.',
 vocab:'arms, hug, reach, lift, stretch, swing',
 teach:'1) Show the card: two arms reaching down from purple sleeves, hands open. Ask what they are doing, then name them: arms.\n2) Find your child’s arms: trace from shoulder to hand. Squeeze gently — the arm is the long part that delivers the hand.\n3) Play the three shapes: arms up like a tree, arms out like an airplane, arms wrapped around your own body like a self-hug. Hold each one for three seconds.\n4) Test the lifting: hand your child a light bag, a cushion, a teddy — the arms carry it across the room. Real cargo makes real pride.\n5) Finish with the sleeve game: push one hand through a sleeve and out the other end — where did it go? Arm in, arm out, arm through: dressing is arms practice.',
 say:'"These are your arms — the long reachers! They lift, they carry, they swing when you walk, and they wrap around for the very best hugs. Up like a tree… out like an airplane… around like a hug! And every sleeve is a tunnel your arm travels through. Ready for cargo?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what are the arms doing — reaching down? Are the hands open? What color are the sleeves? What word sits with the arms?',
 ask:'"What do your arms do?" "Show me all three arm shapes — tree, airplane, hug." "What did your arms carry today?" "What is the tunnel arms go through when we dress?"',
 childSay:'Your child may deliver the three arm shapes as a daily routine, report every carried object as arm-work, or wrap both arms around your neck at pickup with the word attached — hug delivered, by name.',
 real:'Arms work everywhere: reaching the top shelf of the cupboard, carrying the shopping bag one seat over, swinging on the monkey bars, wrapping around a friend who fell down. Name the reach, the carry, the wrap — the levers get their credit.',
 play:'Arm-o-rama circuit: five stations around the room — reach up high and touch the doorframe, out wide and spin like an airplane, around for a self-hug, lift the cushion and carry it one lap, swing both arms like a windmill. Ten seconds each, then swap leadership. Every station is a different arm verb.',
 check:'Ask: "What do arms do? Show me the three shapes. What connects your shoulder to your hand?" The verbs, the shapes and the arm answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Let the hug be part of the lesson, not just a shape — arms learn their job by doing it. And use the sleeve tunnel at dressing time every morning: naming the arm going through turns a daily battle into a body-part game with a winner.',
 safety:'Arms are for reaching, lifting and hugging — never for swinging at people or yanking toys; the rule sticks best when the good jobs are praised loudly. Lift with two arms for heavy things, and hands get help from a grown-up for anything hot or sharp.'},
'legs':{
 title:'Legs',one:'Two legs in purple shorts, built for marching — the engines that carry your child to everything worth reaching.',
 seoTitle:'Legs Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: what legs do, march-and-hop games, the one-leg balance challenge and how legs carry the whole day.',
 intro:'This lesson marches in the biggest movers of all. Legs do the going — walking, running, hopping, kicking, climbing — and the games that name them are the kind your child will want to keep playing long after the lesson ends.',
 what:'Legs are the two long parts between the hips and the feet. They stand, walk, run, jump, hop, kick and climb — every place your child gets to, the legs get them there. At the bottom of each leg is a foot, the part that meets the ground.',
 mean:'Legs are the body’s engines, and feet are their wheels: the legs make the power, the feet steer and land it. Together they carry the whole body through the day — and they are also the strongest muscles your child owns, which is why running feels so good.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: legs walk, run, jump, hop and kick, each leg ends in a foot, one-leg standing is the legs’ balance trick and it takes practice, and legs get care too — trousers on, shoes tied, rest when they are tired.',
 why:'Leg vocabulary and leg strength grow together. The marching, hopping and one-leg games build the coordination that playgrounds demand, and naming the legs means a child can say what hurts, what is tired and what just did something brilliant.',
 dev:'Gross-motor power and coordination, single-leg balance (a genuine milestone skill), movement vocabulary, body-part naming below the waist — often the last to be learned — and the stamina pride of a body that goes and goes.',
 vocab:'legs, walk, run, hop, kick, climb',
 teach:'1) Show the card: two legs in purple shorts, standing ready. Ask what they are for, then name them: legs — the body’s engines.\n2) Find your child’s legs: trace from hip to foot. Bend a knee together and feel how the leg folds — the knee is the hinge in the middle.\n3) Play the engine games: march around the room with high knees, then run on the spot, then a big two-foot jump. The legs are warming up.\n4) Hold the balance challenge: stand on one leg like a flamingo — count how long, try the other leg, then hold a teddy for difficulty. Flamingos are allowed to wobble.\n5) Finish with the carrying idea: everything your child reaches — the park, the slide, the top of the stairs — the legs carried them there. Thank the engines out loud.',
 say:'"These are your legs — the strongest engines you own! They walk, they run, they jump, they kick, they climb. In the middle is your knee, the hinge that folds. Can you march like a soldier? Hop like a bunny? Stand on one leg like a flamingo? Your legs just did the whole circus!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: how many legs are in the picture? What are they wearing — purple shorts? What word sits with the legs? Are they standing still or moving?',
 ask:'"What do your legs do all day?" "Where is your knee — show me the hinge!" "Can you hop on one leg — how many hops?" "What did your legs carry you to today?"',
 childSay:'Your child may march through every doorway for a week, demand flamingo-timing contests with the whole family, or report that legs are the strongest muscles and offer to prove it — engines named, pride installed.',
 real:'Legs are named all day: legs up for the trousers, run to the gate, kick the ball, climb the slide, walk to the bus. The playground is the field trip — every apparatus there is a legs lesson already running.',
 play:'Legs circuit: four stations — march ten steps, five two-foot jumps, three hops on each leg, one long balance on one foot with arms out like wings. Run the circuit twice, then let your child design a station for you. Designing is the highest level of knowing.',
 check:'Ask: "What do legs do? Where is the knee? Show me one-leg balance." The verbs, the hinge and a wobbly flamingo — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Count hops and balance-seconds out loud — the numbers give the game a target and sneak in counting. And expect the flamingo to beat some adults in the family; letting your child win the balance contest is a small price for a body part this loved.',
 safety:'Legs run fast — indoor running lives on rugs, not tiles, and climbing happens with a grown-up in sight. Knees take the falls: patches and plasters are part of the engine’s maintenance, and tired legs are allowed to say so.'},
'see':{
 title:'Seeing',one:'The first of the five senses — how light turns into pictures, and how your child’s eyes learn the world on purpose.',
 seoTitle:'See Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: seeing as one of the body’s five ways to learn, a butterfly landing close, the colour spy game and sun-safety rules.',
 intro:'This lesson opens the five-senses collection with the busiest one. Seeing is how your child takes in most of the world — and this card proves it beautifully: a butterfly has landed right where the eyes can find it.',
 what:'See is what your eyes do: catch light and turn it into pictures inside your head. It is one of the body’s five senses — the five ways to learn about the world — and it is the one working hardest for your child, from the moment the day starts.',
 mean:'Your body has five ways to learn: eyes see, ears hear, nose smells, tongue tastes, skin touches. Seeing is the loudest of the five — most of what a preschooler knows arrived through the eyes. And seeing is a skill, not just a fact: looking closely, like the picture’s butterfly-watcher, is seeing done on purpose.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we see with our eyes, seeing is one of five senses, looking closely is something you can practise — slow down, really look — and eyes need protection: never a look at the sun, bright days get hats and shade.',
 why:'Naming see gives your child the owner’s manual for their busiest sense. It turns look at this into a game they can play alone — spotting, noticing, comparing — and it anchors the five-senses map that every later science lesson, from light to rainbows, will stand on.',
 dev:'Visual attention and observation, the five-senses framework (seeing as one of five), descriptive vocabulary for what the eyes find, sun-safety habits, and the quiet pride of spotting things others miss.',
 vocab:'see, eyes, look, spot, butterfly, observe',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what the eyes are doing — and what has come to visit them. A butterfly, landing near! That is seeing at work: the eyes found it.\n2) Name the five-senses family: eyes see, ears hear, nose smells, tongue tastes, skin touches. Seeing is the first of five ways to learn.\n3) Play colour spy: I see something red… something round… something tiny. The finder must point before shouting the answer — looking before leaping.\n4) Practise looking closely: pick one small thing — a leaf, a button, a toy — and study it for ten slow seconds. What did the eyes catch that a quick look missed?\n5) Set the sun rule with care: the sun is too bright for eyes, ever. Bright days get hats, shade and sunglasses — the eyes do the seeing, the hat does the guarding.',
 say:'"See! Your eyes are one of your five senses — five ways your body learns about the world. The butterfly on this card landed right where the eyes could find it. Let us play: I see something red… can you spot it? Looking closely is seeing done like a scientist."',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what has landed near the eyes — a butterfly? Is it close or far away? What word sits with the picture? What color can you spot in it?',
 ask:'"What do we see with?" "How many senses does your body have — can you name them?" "What did your eyes spot today that nobody else noticed?" "Why do we never look at the sun?"',
 childSay:'Your child may announce the five senses as a memorised team, spot the tiniest ladybug on the walk and demand witnesses, or hold up the sun rule to a grandparent — seeing, named, practised and defended.',
 real:'Seeing is the sense that works everywhere: reading signs, spotting the bus, choosing the best apple, finding a friend in the park. Give the eyes their credit out loud — your eyes found it — and the sense becomes a skill your child knows they own.',
 play:'Spot-the-change: arrange five toys on a tray, let your child look away, secretly remove or swap one, and ask what changed. Three rounds, then swap roles. It is observation training with a mystery plot — and remembering what was there is the hard, valuable part.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we see with? How many senses are there? Show me looking closely." The eyes, the five and a slow deliberate stare — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the colour spy game in the car, the queue and the bath — it is the most portable sense game there is. And when your child spots something first, let them be the spy-caller next round: the finder becomes the teacher, and the sense gets sharpened twice.',
 safety:'The sun rule is absolute: looking straight at the sun — even for a second, even on a hazy day — can damage eyes for life. Hats, shade and sunglasses are the eye’s equipment for bright days, and adults follow the same rule out loud so the copying goes both ways.'},
'hear':{
 title:'Hear',one:'Sound waves, rippling toward an ear — the second of the five senses, and the one that works even in the dark.',
 seoTitle:'Hear Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: hearing as one of the body’s five ways to learn, sound waves on the card, the listening-minute game and volume care.',
 intro:'This lesson tunes the five-senses collection to sound. Hearing is the sense that never closes — working in the dark, through walls, while asleep — and this card draws it beautifully: sound waves rippling out of the picture, straight for an ear.',
 what:'Hear is what your ears do: catch sounds — voices, dogs, rain, music — and send them to the brain to be understood. It is the second of the five senses, and the only one that never switches off for sleep.',
 mean:'Sound travels in invisible waves through the air — the card draws them as ripples — and the ear collects them. Hearing works from every direction at once, even through doors and in the dark, which makes it the body’s early-warning system: the sense that tells you something is happening before you can see what.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we hear with our ears, hearing is the second of the five senses, sounds can be loud or soft, near or far, and ears are delicate — very loud noise right up close hurts them, so volume stays kind and ears get quiet when they need it.',
 why:'Hearing is half of language: every word your child learns arrives through this sense first. The listening games build the attention that stories, music and classroom instructions run on — and a trained ear is also a safety ear, catching the warning shout before the eye finds the danger.',
 dev:'Auditory attention and discrimination, the five-senses framework (hearing as one of five), volume awareness and self-regulation, sound vocabulary (loud, soft, high, low, near, far), and stillness as the price of listening well.',
 vocab:'hear, ears, sound, loud, soft, waves',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what the ripples around the ear might be. Explain: sound travels in waves — invisible, but real — and the ear catches them. That is hearing.\n2) Name the five-senses family again: seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting, touching. Hearing is number two — and it works even with eyes shut.\n3) Prove it: everyone shuts their eyes for five slow seconds and listens. What arrived? Name every sound that came in — the fridge, a car, a voice down the hall.\n4) Play near-and-far: make a sound close to your child’s ear, then the same sound from across the room. Same sound, different size — that is how ears measure distance.\n5) Set the volume rule: ears like sounds at talking level. Shouting straight into an ear, or music at full blast, is too much — quiet is not boring, quiet is ear care.',
 say:'"Hear! Your ears catch sounds in invisible waves — listen: the clock, the bird, my voice. Hearing is one of your five senses, and it never sleeps — your ears work even in the dark! But they are delicate, so sounds stay kind and gentle around them. What can you hear right now, this very second?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what do you see coming from the ear — the purple waves? What word sits with the picture? Is the ear alone or with a face?',
 ask:'"What do we hear with?" "Which sense keeps working while you sleep?" "What sounds can you hear right now?" "What sounds are too loud for ears — and what do we do about it?"',
 childSay:'Your child may freeze mid-play, finger raised, to announce a sound nobody else noticed, ask what made every noise in the house, or turn the music down unprompted with the volume rule quoted — hearing, owned and defended.',
 real:'Hearing fills every part of the day: the kettle, the doorbell, the story at bedtime, the warning call at the road. Give the ears their catches — your ears heard the post arrive — and the invisible sense becomes visible to its owner.',
 play:'The listening minute: sit still together, eyes open or shut, and raise one finger for every new sound heard in one quiet minute. Compare lists afterwards — two ears, two different reports. The stillness is the training; the comparison is the fun.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we hear with? How many senses? What do sound waves look like on our card?" The ears, the five and the ripples — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'The listening minute works in waiting rooms, queues and cars — anywhere stillness is welcome anyway. And model your own listening: when a sound arrives, stop, name it, look for it together. Children learn what attention looks like by watching yours.',
 safety:'Little ears are delicate: keep music and screens at talking volume, no shouting directly into anyone’s ear, and step away from very loud noise — speakers, engines, fireworks — or cover ears when it cannot be avoided. Ringing ears are ears asking for quiet.'},
'smell':{
 title:'Smell',one:'A nose deep in a purple flower — the third of the five senses, and the fastest road from the world to memory.',
 seoTitle:'Smell Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: smelling as one of the body’s five ways to learn, the sniff-and-guess game, warning smells and gentle sniffing rules.',
 intro:'This lesson follows the nose into the five-senses collection. Smelling is the sneakiest sense — working all day without being asked — and this card gives it a worthy subject: a purple flower, sniffed up close, in full bloom.',
 what:'Smell is what your nose does: catch scents riding on the air and report them to the brain. It is the third of the five senses — the nose’s very own way of learning about the world — and the fastest one: a single sniff can announce dinner, rain or a flower before any word is spoken.',
 mean:'Smells travel invisibly, like the sound waves did — tiny pieces of the world drifting through the air — and the nose pulls them up with a sniff. Smell is also the memory sense: a single whiff of a known smell can fetch a whole place back, which is why noses keep grandma’s kitchen better than cameras do.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we smell with our noses, smelling is the third of the five senses, sniffing pulls smells up to be read, some smells are treats and some are warnings — smoke, spoiled food — and sniffing games use food and flowers only.',
 why:'A named nose becomes a working nose, and smell is a safety sense as much as a pleasure one: burnt toast, gas, gone-off milk all announce themselves to whoever is listening. The sniff-and-guess game also builds vocabulary fast — every smell wants its word.',
 dev:'Olfactory attention and vocabulary, the five-senses framework (smelling as one of five), memory-through-scent connections, warning-smell associations, and the gentle sniff technique — small sips of air, not great gulps.',
 vocab:'smell, nose, sniff, scent, fresh, stinky',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what the nose is doing — sniffing a flower! That is the sense called smell, number three of five.\n2) Demonstrate the gentle sniff: a small sip of air through the nose, not a great gulp. Practise on the air in the room — what does here smell like?\n3) Play sniff-and-guess: three covered cups hiding smells — orange peel, a mint leaf, a dab of jam. Eyes shut, sniff, guess, uncover, cheer.\n4) Sort the smells: which were treats and which would be warnings? Name the warning crew — smoke, rotten food, something burnt — smells that mean find a grown-up.\n5) Take the game outside or to the garden: flowers, wet grass after rain, the herb shelf. The world is a smell library, and the nose has a card.',
 say:'"Smell! Your nose catches scents in the air — sniff, sniff, gently, like a small sip. Smelling is one of your five senses, and it is the fastest: one sniff and you know dinner is ready. Some smells are treats — flowers, bananas, clean washing. Some are warnings — smoke means find a grown-up, fast. What does the air smell like right now?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what is the nose sniffing — a purple flower? Is the flower close to the nose or far away? What word sits beside them? What color is the flower?',
 ask:'"What do we smell with?" "Which sense is the fastest — the one that knows dinner before anyone says so?" "What is the best smell in our house?" "What smells mean find a grown-up?"',
 childSay:'Your child may smell every meal from the doorway and announce the menu, report the rain before it arrives, or patrol the flowers with slow deliberate sniffs — the nose, promoted from breathing equipment to chief investigator.',
 real:'Smell reports arrive all day: bread in the bakery aisle, the swimming pool, the rubbish bin, the baby’s head, rain on hot pavement. Name two or three a day — what does your nose say? — and a quiet sense becomes a loud part of the family vocabulary.',
 play:'The smell library: collect three or four safe smells in little cups — lemon peel, cinnamon, a pine needle, a coffee bean — sniff with eyes shut and guess. Swap in new smells weekly. Memory and vocabulary grow with every round, and the guessing stays funny forever.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we smell with? How do we sniff — big gulp or small sip? What smells mean find a grown-up?" The nose, the sip and the warning list — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the smell library to food, herbs and flowers — the safe shelf — and let your child nominate one new smell a week: ownership keeps the game alive. And honour every wrong guess with a sniff-along: comparing notes is where the vocabulary actually gets built.',
 safety:'Sniffing games use food, herbs and flowers only — cleaning sprays, spices held close and anything from under the sink stay off the list, and unknown bottles are find-a-grown-up objects, never sniffed. Gentle sips of air, never deep gulps of anything strong.'},
'fingers':{
 title:'Fingers',one:'An open hand showing all five — the counting set your child was born with, and the finest tools on the body.',
 seoTitle:'Fingers Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: the five-finger count, the wiggle-and-hide games, each finger’s special job and how fingers learn before pencils do.',
 intro:'This lesson counts what your child already carries everywhere. Fingers are the counting set that came free at birth — five per hand, ten in total — and the finest tools the body owns, already at work on buttons, crayons and page corners.',
 what:'Fingers are the five long parts at the end of each hand — a thumb and four fingers. They wiggle, point, grab, pinch and count, and they do the fine work: buttons, zips, page turns, puzzle pieces, pencil grips. Every hand comes with the full set, five per hand.',
 mean:'Fingers are the body’s precision team: the arms reach, the hands hold, and the fingers do the detail. Each one has a speciality — the thumb can touch every other finger, which is why holding anything works; the pointing finger leads the eye; the smallest one sneaks into the tiniest spaces.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: each hand has five fingers, ten on both hands together, they can be counted — which makes them the friendliest maths lesson there is — and each has a job: thumb for grabbing, one for pointing, all of them for wiggling on request.',
 why:'Finger naming is counting’s natural on-ramp: most children count their fingers before they count anything else, and the counting sticks because the objects are always attached. Fine-finger control is also the school-readiness skill underneath every crayon, zip and scissors job to come.',
 dev:'Counting and one-to-one correspondence, fine-motor isolation (moving one finger at a time is genuinely hard), hand preference, pointing as communication, and the finger-play tradition — rhymes and games that train every finger in order.',
 vocab:'fingers, thumb, five, count, wiggle, point',
 teach:'1) Show the card and count the fingers on the open hand: one, two, three, four, five. Then count your child’s real hand to check the set is complete.\n2) Wiggle test: can your child wiggle just one finger, then two, then all five like a piano? Moving one finger alone is a real skill — celebrate every success.\n3) Name the special jobs: the thumb that touches every fingertip, the pointer that shows the way, the little one that fits the small spaces. Try each job out.\n4) Play hide-and-seek fingers: hide all five in a fist, then let one peek out — can your child name which one? Thumb? Pointer? Guessing is half the game.\n5) Give the fingers real work: a button, a zipper, a page to turn, a puzzle piece to place. Fingers learn by doing — and the doing is the lesson.',
 say:'"These are your fingers — five on every hand, ten altogether! They wiggle, they point, they grab, they count. The thumb can touch every single fingertip — try it! And they are the counting set you were born with: one, two, three, four, five. Show me one finger wiggling all by itself!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: how many fingers does the open hand show? Is the thumb among them? What word sits with the hand? Are the fingers spread out or curled?',
 ask:'"How many fingers on one hand? On both hands?" "Can you wiggle just your thumb — now just your pointing finger?" "What is your pointing finger for?" "What did your fingers learn to do this week?"',
 childSay:'Your child may count to ten on fingers at every opportunity, show off a one-finger wiggle like a party trick, or use the pointing finger to lead the whole family to what matters — the counting set, fully operational.',
 real:'Fingers work everywhere: turning book pages, fastening shoes, picking berries, counting out apple slices, pressing doorbells. Name the finger-work as it happens — your fingers buttoned it, your fingers counted it — and the fine team gets its reviews.',
 play:'Finger football: fingers lined up as teams on the table edge, one ball (a soft pom-pom), and flicking only — which finger can shoot the pom-pom across the table? Each finger gets a turn. Precision flicking is finger isolation training in World Cup packaging.',
 check:'Ask: "How many fingers on each hand? Which one touches all the others? Show me a single-finger wiggle." The count, the thumb and the wiggle — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Fingerplays — rhymes with finger actions — are the tradition for good reason: they train isolation, counting and rhythm in one sitting. Learn two or three by heart and keep them for queues and car seats. And never rush pencil grip: fingers that wiggle freely now will hold pencils properly later.',
 safety:'Fingers and doors do not mix — keep them clear of hinges and edges, and fingers and small objects need the choking rule around babies. Fingernails get trimmed by a grown-up; the picking and poking jobs belong to the fingers, not the fingernails.'},
'toes':{
 title:'Toes',one:'Ten little toes in a row — the tickliest counting set on the body, and the grip team hiding inside every shoe.',
 seoTitle:'Toes Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: counting all ten toes, the toe-wiggle games, how toes grip and balance, and sock-and-shoe care as toe care.',
 intro:'This lesson goes to the very end of the body, where the counting and the tickling live. Toes are the fingers’ ground crew — five per foot, ten in total — and the games that name them come with the best sound effects in the whole set.',
 what:'Toes are the five small parts at the end of each foot — ten altogether. They wiggle, grip, push and balance: every step your child takes, the toes press the ground and hold on. The big toe is the boss one; the littlest ones ride along.',
 mean:'Toes are the body’s grip team and its balancing crew. Press your toes down and you can stand taller; spread them and you can balance wider. They are also the most ticklish address on the body, which is why toe games come with laughter built in — and why counting them sticks better than counting anything else.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: each foot has five toes, ten on both feet, the big toe is the leader, toes help balance and grip — try picking up a sock with your toes — and they get care too: socks when cold, shoes that fit, toenails trimmed by a grown-up.',
 why:'Toe counting is the second verse of finger counting, and it locks in numbers to ten. Knowing the toes also gives your child words for the shoe shop, the bath and the doctor — and the toe-wiggle games build the foot strength that barefoot running and balance need.',
 dev:'Counting to ten across two feet, toe isolation and strength (wiggling one toe is the hard mode), balance and grip awareness, body-part naming at the extremities, and the tickle-and-giggle bonding that keeps the lesson requested.',
 vocab:'toes, ten, wiggle, tickle, grip, balance',
 teach:'1) Show the card and count the toes: one to five on the card, then one to five on your child’s real foot, then both feet — all the way to ten.\n2) Wiggle test: can all ten wiggle at once? Can just the big toes — the boss toes — wiggle on their own? Try it together; it is harder than it sounds.\n3) Test the grip: lay a sock or a small cloth on the floor and pick it up with toes only. Ten toes, one sock, no hands — the room will roar.\n4) Try the balance trick: stand tall and press the toes into the floor — feel how the toes hold you up. Then lift the toes and feel the wobble arrive.\n5) Finish with toe care: socks when cold, shoes that fit, toenails cut by a grown-up. Toes work inside shoes all day; they get air-time at bath time.',
 say:'"These are your toes — ten little ones, five on each foot! They wiggle, they grip, they balance you. The big toe is the boss — try to wiggle just the bosses! And they are the tickliest spot on your whole body, which is why we count them like this: one, two, three, four, five… and here comes the tickle!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: how many toes can you count on the feet? Are they big, little, or both? What word sits above them? Are the toes lined up in a row?',
 ask:'"How many toes on each foot — and altogether?" "Which toe is the boss?" "Can you pick up a sock with your toes?" "Who is the tickliest person in this family?"',
 childSay:'Your child may demand the toe-counting-with-tickle finish at every nappy change of a sibling, walk around gripping a sock in each foot, or announce the boss toes at the shoe shop — ten counted, grip tested, laughter banked.',
 real:'Toes appear at bath time, the shoe shop, the beach and the paddling pool — sand between toes is a whole report. Name the grip at work: your toes held the ladder, your toes balanced the beam — the ground crew gets the review.',
 play:'Toe tug-of-war: sit facing your child, feet touching, and hook toes together gently — pull! Whoever unhooks loses. It is toe strength, giggle fuel and a fair fight the small ones often win. Follow with the sock pickup championship for the quiet version.',
 check:'Ask: "How many toes altogether? Which one is the boss? Show me a toe grip." The ten, the big toe and the sock pickup — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the tickle at the end of the count — the numbers buy the tickle, which keeps the counting requested. And give toes barefoot minutes on safe surfaces: grip and balance grow strongest when the ground crew meets the ground directly.',
 safety:'Toenails get trimmed by a grown-up, straight across, and shoes that fit beat shoes that will-fit-eventually — squeezed toes complain for years. Barefoot play happens on checked floors and clean grass; the toilet-paper-tube rule applies to anything toe-sized near babies.'},
'skin':{
 title:'Skin',one:'The covering that feels everything — warm and cool, soft and rough — and the quiet fifth sense doing the touching.',
 seoTitle:'Skin Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: skin as the touch sense and the body’s cover, the warm-and-cool game, its warning job and gentle care.',
 intro:'This lesson meets the sense that goes everywhere the body goes. Skin covers the whole body — arms, cheeks, the tip of the nose — and it is doing two jobs at once: keeping the body safely in, and reporting what the world feels like.',
 what:'Skin is the covering that wraps the whole body — and it is the touch sense itself. It reports warm and cool, soft and hard, bumpy and smooth, dry and wet, and it sends the warnings too: too hot, too sharp, something crawling.',
 mean:'Skin is the body’s largest sense organ, and the only one worn everywhere. Touch is the fifth of the five senses — eyes see, ears hear, nose smells, tongue tastes, and skin does the feeling. It is also the body’s coat: it keeps the inside in, the weather out, and heals itself when it gets a scrape.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: skin covers the whole body and it is how we feel things, touch is the fifth sense, skin reports the world — warm water, cool tiles, a soft cat, a scratchy jumper — and it needs care: washes, plasters for scrapes, and sun hats on bright days.',
 why:'Touch is the first sense a baby ever used — held, wrapped, rocked — and it stays the comfort sense for life: a hug is skin talking. Naming skin gives your child words for comfort and for warning — this jumper scratches, that radiator burns — and both kinds of sentence matter.',
 dev:'Tactile vocabulary (warm, cool, soft, rough, smooth, bumpy), the five-senses framework (touch as the fifth), body coverage awareness — skin is everywhere — and the self-care links: washing, plasters, sun protection.',
 vocab:'skin, touch, warm, cool, soft, scratchy',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what the close-up picture might be. Reveal it: skin — the covering on your whole body, and the feeling sense itself.\n2) Prove the coverage: skin on the arm, skin on the cheek, skin on the nose, skin on the tummy. It is everywhere you look — the body’s own coat.\n3) Play the two-bowl test: one bowl of warm water, one of cool. One hand in each, then report — warm, cool! The skin told you which was which, instantly.\n4) Read textures: a soft blanket, a bumpy towel, a smooth table, a scratchy jumper. Touch each and say the word the skin sent.\n5) Finish with skin care: washes keep it clean, plasters look after scrapes, sun hats guard it on bright days. The coat does a lot — it earns its maintenance.',
 say:'"This is skin — it covers you everywhere! Your arms, your cheeks, the tip of your nose: all skin. It is your touch sense, the fifth of your five senses — it feels warm and cool, soft and scratchy, and it tells your brain everything. Skin is also your coat: it keeps you in and the world out. Take care of your coat!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what does the close-up show — a patch of skin? What word sits with it? Is the skin smooth or bumpy in the picture? Where does your own skin show right now?',
 ask:'"What covers your whole body?" "Which sense is skin — number five of how many?" "What did your skin feel today — warm? cool? soft?" "How do we take care of skin?"',
 childSay:'Your child may report textures all day — the rug is fluffy, the window is cold — ask for a plaster with dignity when a scrape arrives, or announce that the bath water is warm and the tiles are cool, both confirmed by the fifth sense.',
 real:'Skin reports arrive constantly: bath water tested with a toe, the cat’s fur, granny’s scratchy scarf, the sun-warmed slide. Name the reports as they come — your skin told you that — and the touch sense becomes a working vocabulary instead of a feeling without words.',
 play:'The texture trail: line up four safe textures along the floor — blanket, bubble wrap, towel, smooth cushion — and walk them barefoot, naming each underfoot feeling. Then do it with eyes shut. The feet have skin too, and the trail proves it.',
 check:'Ask: "What covers your body? Which sense is it? What did your skin feel today?" The covering, the fifth and a texture or two — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep a texture basket — fabric squares, a sponge, a pinecone, a smooth stone — and rotate it weekly; the skin learns like the ears do, by comparison. And treat every plaster ceremony as skin science: the coat got a rip, the plaster is the repair, the body heals it itself.',
 safety:'Skin reports pain for a reason — hot things, sharp things and broken glass are look-don’t-touch, and the warning gets obeyed, not tested. Scrapes get washed and covered; burns get cold water and a grown-up, fast. Sun hats and sunscreen are the coat’s weather gear.'},
'tongue':{
 title:'Tongue',one:'The pink muscle behind the smile — taster of food, shaper of words and the silliest thing to stick out on purpose.',
 seoTitle:'Tongue Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: the tongue’s two jobs, the up-out-side-to-side workout, its tasting work with the five senses and the peek-out game.',
 intro:'This lesson meets the body’s show-off. The tongue hides all day behind the teeth and then, on request, peeks out — and between the silliness it does two serious jobs: tasting every bite and shaping every word.',
 what:'The tongue is the pink muscle that lives inside the mouth. It tastes the food, moves the food where chewing needs it, and shapes the sounds that become words. It can go up, out, side to side and curl — the busiest muscle your child owns.',
 mean:'The tongue is a muscle that never stops: it stirs every bite, checks every temperature, presses every sound into shape — the same tongue says llama and lollipop — and then cleans the teeth afterwards. It is also where the taste sense lives: sweet, salty, sour and more, all reported from this one pink floor of the mouth.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: the tongue lives inside the mouth, it tastes the food — one of the five senses happens right here — it helps make every word, it can peek out and go back in on request, and it stays inside for eating and talking: peeking is a game, not a habit.',
 why:'The tongue is where eating meets speaking — the two hardest jobs a preschooler’s body does. Playing with it builds the oral-motor control that clear speech runs on, and naming it gives your child the word for the spot that burns on hot soup and tingles on pineapple.',
 dev:'Oral-motor control (up, out, side to side — the tongue workout), taste awareness as one of the five senses, speech-sound practice through play, and the giggle factor that keeps a hidden body part in the vocabulary.',
 vocab:'tongue, taste, lick, words, pink, muscle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what is peeking out of the open mouth — the tongue! Name it, then find it in a mirror.\n2) Play the peek game: tongue out, tongue in, tongue up to the nose, tongue side to side. Four directions, four giggles — the tongue workout.\n3) Name the jobs: it tastes the food, moves the food for the teeth, and shapes the words. Say la-la-la and tip-tap the tongue behind the top teeth.\n4) Try the lick test: lick your lips like something yummy just happened. Then stick the tongue out for the wind to dry — that is how dogs do it.\n5) Finish with the manners: the tongue peeks for games and stays in for eating and talking. Peek now… and back in it goes, like a tortoise into its shell.',
 say:'"This is your tongue — the pink muscle inside your mouth! It tastes your food, it moves your food, and it shapes every word you say: la-la-la, feel it tap! It can peek out — bleh! — and hide back in, quick as a tortoise. The tongue is the busiest, silliest, strongest muscle you own."',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what is sticking out of the open mouth? What word sits with the picture? Is the tongue out or hidden? Can you make your own do the same thing?',
 ask:'"Where does your tongue live?" "What does it taste — name the flavours it knows!" "What sound does la-la-la make with the tongue?" "When does the tongue peek out — and when does it stay in?"',
 childSay:'Your child may run the four-direction tongue workout in the car mirror, report that llama and lollipop come from the same place, or peek the tongue and vanish it with tortoise speed — the show-off muscle, fully rehearsed.',
 real:'Tongues work at every meal and every sentence: stirring soup, checking for loose teeth, rolling Rs, licking an ice cream on a hot day. The dentist asks for it too — lift the tongue, says the mirror — and now your child knows exactly which part that is.',
 play:'Tongue aerobics: out-and-in for ten, up-to-the-nose for five, side-to-side for ten, then the big finish — can the tongue touch the chin? Points for trying. It is the oral-motor workout that speech therapists charge for, delivered as a comedy routine.',
 check:'Ask: "Where does the tongue live? Name its jobs. Show me up, out and side to side." The mouth, the three jobs and the workout — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'The tongue workout doubles as speech practice for tricky sounds — L, T, D all live there — so keep the aerobics playful and short. And if the peeking outlasts the game, the tortoise rule is your friend: the tongue goes back in its shell for eating and talking.',
 safety:'The tongue stays in its own mouth — no licking friends, pets or shopping trolleys, and no touching it with fingers or toys. Hot food burns tongues first: the blowing-before-tasting rule applies here most of all, and very cold treats bite their own warning.'},
'touch':{
 title:'Touch',one:'The doing-word of the skin — a hand laid gently on something soft and blue, reading the world at fingertip speed.',
 seoTitle:'Touch Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: touch as the skin’s way of learning, the soft-smooth-bumpy hunt, gentle-hands practice and the look-don’t-touch list.',
 intro:'This lesson gives the fifth sense its doing-word. Skin does all the feeling, and touch is the word for it in action — and the card slows everything right down: one hand, laid on something soft and blue, taking its time.',
 what:'Touch is what skin does: it reaches out and reports what the world feels like — soft, smooth, bumpy, warm, cool, squishy. It is the fifth of the five senses, and the only one the whole body carries: hands, cheeks, toes — wherever skin goes, touch goes too.',
 mean:'Touch is the sense of doing: the other four mostly take in, but touch has to reach out and meet the world first. That meeting is how babies learned everything at the start, and it is still how three-year-olds check a claim — one hand on it says more than ten words about it.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we touch with our skin, touching is the fifth sense, the hands are its best tools — and gentle is the rule: slow hands, soft landings, while hot or sharp things are for looking at and naming, not touching.',
 why:'Naming touch turns feeling into vocabulary — the rug is soft, the orange is bumpy, the window is cool — and those texture words power preschool talk. It is also where gentle hands get trained: a child who can name the sense can practise the manners that come with it.',
 dev:'Tactile words (soft, smooth, bumpy, warm, cool), the fifth-sense framework (touch as skin’s job), gentle-hands self-regulation, slow deliberate reaching instead of grabbing, and texture discrimination through the fingers.',
 vocab:'touch, skin, soft, smooth, bumpy, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card and slow everything down: what is the hand doing — resting, gently, on something soft? That careful landing is the whole lesson: touch.\n2) Name the tool: skin does the feeling, and the hands are its busiest workers. Press a palm flat on the table, then on a cushion — two reports, one sense.\n3) Play the texture hunt: find one soft thing, one smooth thing, one bumpy thing in the room. Touch each slowly and say its word out loud.\n4) Practise the gentle rule: reach out slowly, land like a butterfly, no grabbing. Try it on a teddy, on your own arm, on the rug.\n5) Set the boundary kindly: touch means gentle, and some things say look-don’t-touch — radiators, scissors, the stove. Those get pointed at and named, never handled.',
 say:'"Touch! Your skin is the feeler — it tells you soft, smooth, bumpy, warm, cool. Reach out slowly, land gently, and hear what your hands find. Some things we touch, and some things — hot and sharp ones — we only look at and name. Show me your gentlest touch!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what is the hand doing — resting or grabbing? What color is the thing under the hand — blue? Does the hand look gentle or fast? Where does the word touch sit?',
 ask:'"What do we touch with?" "Which sense is touch — the fifth of how many?" "What is the softest thing in this room?" "What things in our house are look-don’t-touch?"',
 childSay:'Your child may spend an afternoon patting the furniture and filing reports — bumpy, smooth, fluffy — land butterfly hands on every head in the family, or point at the stove and recite the look-don’t-touch list, finger and all.',
 real:'Touch reports arrive all day: the bumpy wall on the stairs, the smooth apple, the cold tap, the soft towel. Name what the skin sent — your hands found it! — and the fifth sense becomes a vocabulary your child uses without being asked.',
 play:'Mystery bag: hide three household objects — a spoon, a sponge, a small ball — inside a pillowcase. One hand in, no peeking: read it, guess, pull it out, check. Swap the cast daily. It is touch discrimination, patience and naming practice in one old sock.',
 check:'Ask: "What does the skin do? Which sense number is touch? Show me a gentle landing." The feeling, the fifth and a butterfly hand — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the texture hunt to three objects a day — three good words beat ten rushed ones. And narrate your own gentle touches out loud — patting the cat, smoothing the duvet — because children copy the hands they watch, not the hands they are told about.',
 safety:'Touch has a look-don’t-touch list and it is real: hot oven doors, radiators, irons, scissors, knives and broken glass get pointed at and named, not handled. Gentle hands with pets and babies too — slow landings, no squeezing — and a grown-up for anything unknown.'},
'taste':{
 title:'Taste',one:'The mouth’s own sense — a tongue out and a lollipop in, sorting the whole world into sweet, salty, sour and yum.',
 seoTitle:'Taste Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: taste as the tongue’s sense, the three-flavour game, how taste and smell work as a team and the food-only rule.',
 intro:'This lesson is the snack-time sense. Taste lives on the tongue, and the card catches it in action: mouth open, tongue out, a lollipop mid-test. Of all the five senses this is the one with a menu — and the best snacks for practising it.',
 what:'Taste is what the tongue does: it checks every bite and reports the flavour — sweet, salty, sour, bitter and yummy-all-over. It is the fourth of the five senses, and it never works alone: the nose smells every bite too, which is why food goes quiet when a blocked nose arrives.',
 mean:'Taste is the safest sense to train, because the laboratory is the kitchen and the equipment is lunch. The tongue carries thousands of taste-spotters, and the nose does the fine detail — hold your nose mid-bite and everything goes mysterious. Together they turn eating into science, three times a day.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we taste with our tongue, tasting is the fourth sense, flavours have names — sweet, salty, sour — and taste has rules: only food, only with a grown-up’s okay; hot things wait until a grown-up has checked them.',
 why:'A named sense gets used on purpose. Children who can say sweet and sour describe their world, try new foods as a game instead of a battle, and carry the rule that keeps the sense safe: the tongue tastes food — and only food — when a grown-up has said yes.',
 dev:'Flavour vocabulary (sweet, salty, sour, yum), the five-senses framework (taste as the fourth), taste-and-smell teamwork awareness, trying new foods through game framing, and the food-only rule that keeps little mouths safe.',
 vocab:'taste, tongue, sweet, salty, sour, yum',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what is going on — a lollipop test, tongue out, mid-taste! Name it: taste, the fourth of the five senses.\n2) Find the taste-spotters: the tongue lives in the mouth and checks every bite. Say ahh and spot it in a mirror — the pink floor where flavour lands.\n3) Play the three-flavour game at snack time: a slice of banana (sweet), a cracker (salty), a squeeze of apple (sour). One small bite each, one word each.\n4) Show the teamwork: hold your nose while tasting — suddenly everything is mysterious. Nose and tongue are a team, and food tastes bigger when both report.\n5) Set the rules with a smile: the tongue tastes food and only food, a grown-up says when it is tasting time, and hot things wait until a grown-up has checked them. Blow first, then taste.',
 say:'"Taste! Your tongue knows flavours — sweet like banana, salty like crackers, sour like apple, yum like everything. Tasting is one of your five senses, number four! And it has rules: food only, grown-up first, and hot things get checked and blown before the tongue says hello. Ready to be a taste scientist?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what is being tasted — a lollipop? Is the tongue out or in? What word sits with the picture? Has your child ever given a lollipop the full taste test?',
 ask:'"What do we taste with?" "Which sense is taste — the fourth of how many?" "What did you taste today that was sweet? Sour?" "What is the rule for hot food?"',
 childSay:'Your child may grade every meal by flavour — sweet winner, sour surprise — hold a nose closed mid-dinner to prove the teamwork, or announce the rules at the table: food only, grown-up first, blow before taste.',
 real:'Taste runs through every day: the sweet bite of porridge with honey, salty lips after the pool, the sour pucker at a lemon, the yum at the end of a birthday cake. Give each flavour its word at the table and the sense becomes family vocabulary.',
 play:'The taste-test station: three tiny cups with a drop or crumb in each — sweet, salty, sour — eyes shut, one taste each, guess which is which. Then swap roles: your child sets the cups and quizzes you. Being the scientist is half the learning.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we taste with? Name the flavours. What is the rule for hot things?" The tongue, the three words and the blow-first answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the tastes tiny — one crumb, one drop — and let your child spit out anything they dislike without a fuss; brave tasting grows where there is no pressure. Sour is the crowd-pleaser: one lemon face earns a whole week of requests.',
 safety:'Taste is the one sense that comes with a grown-up on duty: grown-ups wash the food and cut it into safe pieces, only food goes on the tongue, and hot things — soup, toast, cocoa — wait until a grown-up checks the temperature and says blow-then-taste. Anything off the floor, out of a pocket or off a plant is not food, whatever it looks like.'},
'hair':{
 title:'Hair',one:'The crown of the head — brushed every morning, ruffled at every opportunity, and different on every single person your child knows.',
 seoTitle:'Hair Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: where hair grows, the brush-and-pat games, hair colours and curls as a first friendly chat about differences, and hair care.',
 intro:'This lesson climbs to the top of the head. Hair is the body part your child meets most often without naming it — brushed, washed, trimmed and ruffled daily — and the card gives it a face: a boy with a dark crop, ready for the pat test.',
 what:'Hair is the stuff that grows from the skin on top of the head — and only there, which is what gives it an address. It can be dark or light, curly or straight, long or short, thick or fine. It keeps warm the head it lives on, and it is one of the first things people notice about each other.',
 mean:'Hair is the body part that is never hidden and never the same: every child in the classroom owns a different version. That makes it the gentlest possible first conversation about differences — my hair, your hair, her curls, his crop — and the easiest place to practise the big idea that different is normal.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: hair grows on top of the head, out of the skin, and it comes in every colour and curl — straight, curly, dark, fair, long, short. It needs a little care: brush and wash, and a grown-up for trims. Everyone’s hair is different, and everyone’s is good.',
 why:'Naming hair gives your child the word for a daily routine — the brush, the wash, the hat — and for the noticing they already do. Hair is also where children first meet difference and sameness side by side: comparing hair kindly is a small daily rehearsal for a much bigger skill.',
 dev:'Body-part naming above the face line, descriptive vocabulary (colour, curl, length, texture), comparing and contrasting as early thinking, gentle hands in hair-care routines, and the first friendly chat about how people differ.',
 vocab:'hair, brush, curls, straight, trim, dark',
 teach:'1) Show the card and spot the hair: dark, drawn neatly over the boy’s head. Then find your child’s hair — same address, different style.\n2) Pat test: pat your own hair, pat your child’s — soft? fuzzy? smooth? Hair answers differently on every head, and the patting is the point.\n3) Play the mirror game: brush it smooth, ruffle it wild, spike it up, sweep it over one eye and giggle. Hair does both — tidy and wild — and so does the day.\n4) Compare kindly: whose hair in the family is curly? Whose is straight? Who has dark and who has fair? Every answer is right, and every difference is interesting.\n5) Finish with care: the brush visits every morning, the wash happens at bath time, and trims are a grown-up’s job — scissors and heads only ever meet at the barber’s.',
 say:'"This is hair — it grows on top of your head! Yours is yours, and everyone’s is different: curly, straight, dark, fair, long, short. Hair gets brushed in the morning, washed at bath time, and trimmed by a grown-up when it gets long. Give yours a pat — is it soft? Whose hair in this family curls?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: whose head is this — a boy’s? What color is his hair? Is it dark or light? What word sits with the picture? How is your own hair the same and different?',
 ask:'"Where does hair grow?" "What does your hair feel like — soft? curly? spiky?" "Who in our family has the curliest hair? The darkest?" "Who cuts hair when it gets long?"',
 childSay:'Your child may compare hair at every opportunity — the postman’s, the dog’s, the teddy’s — report the brush results each morning, or announce that everyone’s hair is different and everyone’s is good, both parts word for word.',
 real:'Hair comes up all day: hats on and off, wind at the park, bath-night washes, the haircut chair with its cape and scissors. Name the routine — brush, wash, trim — and the morning hair battle gets a body-part reason attached.',
 play:'The hair salon: set up a chair, a towel cape, a soft brush and a mirror, and let your child style every teddy and doll in the house — brush, pat, announce, repeat. Then swap: your child is the barber, you are the customer, and the gentle-hands practice goes both ways.',
 check:'Ask: "What grows on top of your head? What does the brush do? Who does the trims?" The place, the routine and the grown-up answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep comparisons kind and specific — curly like grandpa, red like the neighbour — because hair is where children rehearse their first difference-talk. And let your child hold the brush even if the result is wild: a hand that brushes its own hair is a hand learning self-care.',
 safety:'Hair care has sharp edges: scissors and clippers are grown-up tools, and trims happen at the barber’s or with a still, sitting child and steady hands. Small clips and bobbles stay away from babies, and brushing starts at the ends and works up — gentle, not tugging.'},
'chin':{
 title:'Chin',one:'The small ledge at the bottom of the face — tricky to spot, easy to find with one tucked finger, and the home of every thinking pose.',
 seoTitle:'Chin Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: where the chin is, the arrow that finds it, the thinker pose, chin-up language and how the chin shows feelings.',
 intro:'This lesson finds the smallest body part on the card — and the trickiest one to point at. The chin hides below the mouth, stars in every thinking pose, and the card settles the where-is-it question with an arrow aimed exactly at the right spot.',
 what:'The chin is the little bony ledge at the very bottom of the face, just under the mouth and the lips. It is what rests on a hand in a thinking pose, what tucks down when a jumper is pulled over a head, and what juts out when someone decides to be brave.',
 mean:'The chin is a landmark more than a worker: the face has a map, and the chin is its bottom edge. Knowing it completes the map — forehead, eyes, nose, mouth, chin — and gives your child words for the way feelings show: chin up for brave, chin tucked for shy, chin on a hand for thinking hard.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: the chin is the bony bit at the bottom of the face, just under the lips, found by tucking a finger under it — not the neck, not the cheek — and it moves with feelings: up for brave, down for thinking. Most children point at the neck first, which is what the arrow on the card is for.',
 why:'The chin is usually the last face part to be named, and naming it finishes the face map. It also carries real language — chin up, chin rest, chin strap — and the finder’s pride is real, because a part this small, found this precisely, is a genuine achievement for a three-year-old finger.',
 dev:'Face-map completion (forehead to chin), spatial language (under, below, bottom edge), precision pointing as fine-motor practice, feeling-words linked to chin poses, and the mirror work that builds self-recognition.',
 vocab:'chin, face, arrow, think, tuck, under',
 teach:'1) Show the card and follow the arrow with one finger: where does it land? At the very bottom of the face — the chin. Name it, then hunt it down in a mirror.\n2) Play the finder: tuck a finger under your child’s chin — there it is! Watch out for the neck trick: the chin sits above that, just under the lip.\n3) Strike the thinker pose: finger on chin, eyebrows down, hmmmmm. Big thoughts need a chin, and the sillier the pose the better it sticks.\n4) Do the chin feelings: chin up — brave! Chin tucked — sneaky! Chin resting on both hands — bored, or thinking about snacks. Your child guesses each one.\n5) Complete the face map together: forehead, eyes, nose, mouth, chin — bottom of the map reached. Tap each one in order, faster and faster.',
 say:'"This is your chin — the very bottom of your face, just under your lips! Tuck a finger under it: there! It is where big thoughts happen — finger on chin, hmmmm — and where brave happens too: chin up! Forehead, eyes, nose, mouth, chin: your whole face, mapped from top to bottom!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: where is the arrow pointing — at the chin? Whose face is it? Is the chin at the top of the face or the bottom? Can you find your own chin without a mirror?',
 ask:'"Where is your chin — show me with one finger!" "What does the chin rest on in a thinking pose?" "Which face part sits just above the chin?" "How does a brave chin stand?"',
 childSay:'Your child may answer every question from here on in the thinker pose, correct a sibling who points at the neck — no, the chin, the arrow says so — or march the face map at top speed: forehead, eyes, nose, mouth, chin!',
 real:'Chins come up at the dentist — lift the chin for me — in the jumper tug when a head gets stuck halfway, and in every chins-up at the doctor’s. The face map ends at the chin, and now your child knows the last stop.',
 play:'Chin-and-balance races: walk a line on the floor with a soft toy balanced on the chin — wobbling allowed, giggling guaranteed. Or pass a balloon chin-to-chin with a partner: no hands, chins only. It is chin awareness, posture control and comedy in one game.',
 check:'Ask: "Where is the chin? What sits just above it? Show me the thinker pose." The bottom-of-the-face answer, the lips and a thoughtful hmm — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If the finger keeps landing on the neck, go back to the mirror and trace the jaw together — bottom of the face, under the lips, a small bony ledge. And let the thinker pose become a family joke: a child who strikes it mid-conversation is a child who owns the word.',
 safety:'Chins take the fall in trips — they land first when feet race wheels — so every chin bump gets a grown-up’s look, a cold cloth and a cuddle. Helmet chin straps get buckled before wheels roll: the strap lives under the chin, and so does the rule — helmet on, strap clicked, then ride.'},
'cheeks':{
 title:'Cheeks',one:'The soft round pair on a smiling face — pat-ready, puff-ready, and the official landing strip for grandma kisses.',
 seoTitle:'Cheeks Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Body Parts (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: where the cheeks are, the pat-and-puff games, how cheeks show feelings and weather, and why a gentle pat calms a wobble.',
 intro:'This lesson lands on the softest part of the face. Cheeks sit below the eyes and beside the smile; they puff, blush and hold kisses — and the card catches them at their best: rosy, round and grinning.',
 what:'The cheeks are the two soft round parts of the face, below the eyes and either side of the mouth. They puff out for blowing, lift high for a real smile, go rosy in the cold and hold still for kisses. Everyone has two, and no two pairs match.',
 mean:'Cheeks are the face’s weather report and its mood ring: pink in the cold, flushed after running, puffed mid-bubble, dimpled mid-giggle. They are also the place people reach for first — a kiss lands there, a gentle pat calms there — which makes them the face’s comfort corner.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: cheeks are the soft parts either side of the smile, they puff like balloons for blowing, they show pink in cold weather and after running, and gentle pats and kisses land on them. Cheeks get gentle hands: no pinching, no squeezing.',
 why:'Cheek words carry feelings — rosy, puffed, dimpled — and cold-weather talk: hat on, cheeks pink. Naming them gives your child the vocabulary for how a face shows what it feels, and the pat-pat game doubles as a calm-down trick that works on wobbly days.',
 dev:'Face-map vocabulary below the eyes, descriptive feeling-words (rosy, puffed, dimpled), oral-motor play through cheek puffing, gentle-touch practice, and the soothing ritual of the calm pat.',
 vocab:'cheeks, rosy, puff, pat, smile, pink',
 teach:'1) Show the card and find the cheeks: two soft rounds, one on each side of the smile. Then find your child’s — count them: one, two.\n2) Pat gently, both of you: pat-pat, like a tiny drum on dough. Gentle is the rule — cheeks are for soft pats, not pinches.\n3) Play the balloon game: puff both cheeks up full, then let the air out with a pbbbbfft. Try holding the puff while the other counts — three seconds is the record attempt.\n4) Test the weather report: puff and blow — cheeks working. Pretend to shiver — cheeks going pink. Big smile — cheeks lifting high. The face talks with its cheeks.\n5) Finish with the landing: where do grandma kisses arrive? On the cheeks! One kiss, one pat, and the cheeks are officially the family’s comfort corner.',
 say:'"These are your cheeks — one here, one here, soft as pillows! They puff up like balloons — pfff — they go pink in the cold, they lift up when you smile, and they catch kisses. Pat them gently, pat-pat. Can you puff them up big? Now let the air out — pbbbbfft!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: is the face smiling? What color are the cheeks — rosy? How many cheeks can you count? What word sits with the picture? Can you puff your own like balloons?',
 ask:'"Where are your cheeks — show me both!" "What do cheeks do when we blow? When we smile?" "What color do cheeks go in the cold?" "Who lands kisses on your cheeks?"',
 childSay:'Your child may patrol the family for cheek-patting rights — gently, gently — puff on demand and deflate with full sound effects, or report the weather on every face: pink cheeks, cold day, hat needed.',
 real:'Cheeks report all day: rosy after the slide, pink at the bus stop, puffed over a bowl of bubble mix, lifted at the door when you come home. Name the report — your cheeks say you had fun — and the face gains a weather channel.',
 play:'The cheek orchestra: everyone puffs at the count of three, holds for two, and deflates on the conductor’s signal — louder, longer, higher on request. Then the slow movement: gentle pat-pat on your own cheeks, softer and softer until the hands rest. It is breath control disguised as a brass section.',
 check:'Ask: "Where are the cheeks? What do they do when we blow? How do we touch them — gently or pinchy?" The pair, the puff and the gentle answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'The slow pat-pat is a real calming tool on wobbly days — softer, slower, quieter until the storm passes; children borrow the calm from the rhythm. And let blowing practice live here too: puffed cheeks are the first step toward candles, whistles and bubbles.',
 safety:'Cheeks are for gentle: no pinching, no squeezing, no grabbing faces, however cute the moment — the rule is said kindly and often. Cold, windy days chap little cheeks, so hats, scarves and a dab of cream do the guarding, and very sore or swollen cheeks get a grown-up’s check.'},
'look':{
 title:'Look',one:'The doing-word for eyes — a boy bending down close to a red ball, showing that looking is something you do with your whole body.',
 seoTitle:'Look Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: look as the eyes’ doing-word, looking close and looking far, the freeze-and-stare game and the still-feet rule.',
 intro:'This lesson puts the eyes to work. See is the sense; look is the job — bending down, getting close, taking your time. The card shows it exactly: a boy folded low over a red ball, close enough to study every inch of it.',
 what:'Look is what eyes do on purpose: point them at something and study it. Looking is slower than seeing — the whole body helps, bending down, holding still, getting close. It is the eyes’ doing-word, and it is the difference between a glance and a real investigation.',
 mean:'See happens whether you choose it or not; look is the chosen version — attention aimed on purpose. That aiming is the first scientific skill your child owns: freeze, focus, study, report. The card’s bent knees say it plainly: looking closely is body work, and it takes practise.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we look with our eyes, looking means slowing down and getting close — bend, freeze, study — it is the doing-word for the seeing sense, and moving feet get eyes up: eyes study best when the body is still, so feet stop first, then eyes work.',
 why:'Look is one of the most-used words in a preschool day — look at this, look where you are going, look at me! — and a child who owns the word owns the game of noticing. Looking closely feeds every later skill: puzzles, books, patterns, science, and the find-your-shoes speed run at the door.',
 dev:'Visual attention and deliberate focus, body-stillness as a condition for looking, action-word (verb) vocabulary, near-and-far looking ranges, and observation as the first step of the science habit.',
 vocab:'look, eyes, close, study, spot, freeze',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the boy doing — bending down, low and slow, to look at something? And at what? A red ball, worth studying.\n2) Copy the pose: bend down low together and pick one thing to study — a toy, a shell, a pattern on the rug. Ten slow seconds, then report: what did the close look find?\n3) Play look-up-look-down: look up high, look down low, look behind you, look between your knees. Freeze after each one — looking bodies are still bodies.\n4) Try near and far: hold a toy at nose distance and study it, then set it across the room and study it again. Same toy, two reports — looking has ranges.\n5) Set the feet rule: when feet are moving, eyes go up; when eyes want to study, feet stop first. Look while still, walk while watching — that is the safe way round.',
 say:'"Look! Your eyes have a doing-word, and this is it: look. Bend down slow, hold still, and really study something — a red ball, a bug, a pattern. What did the long look find that the quick one missed? And remember: feet stop first, then eyes work. Show me your best freeze-and-look!"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what is the boy doing — bending down? What is he studying — a red ball? Is he up close or far off? What word sits with the picture? What was the last thing you bent down to look at?',
 ask:'"What do we look with?" "How is looking different from just seeing?" "Show me a freeze-and-look — what did you find?" "What is the rule for feet and eyes when we walk?"',
 childSay:'Your child may drop into the freeze-and-look pose mid-pavement — ladybug under study, feet already stopped — issue look! commands at every window, or explain the feet rule to a running friend: stop first, then eyes.',
 real:'Look runs the whole day: look both ways at the kerb, look for the missing shoe, look at this drawing, look how tall the tower got. Each command lands better on a child who knows that looking is a skill — and that feet stop before eyes start.',
 play:'The close-study gallery: collect three small treasures — a pinecone, a shell, a big leaf — and give each a full slow look, ten seconds minimum, then a report of three findings. Line them up on a shelf as the gallery. Fresh treasures weekly; the study habit grows with the collection.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we look with? Show me a freeze-and-look. What do feet do first?" The eyes, the pose and the stop answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Give the study time room to be slow — the whole point is the long look, and three-year-olds need seconds to find the details. And join in: study one thing yourself and report out loud, because a child who watches a grown-up look closely learns what close looking looks like.',
 safety:'Looking has a moving-feet rule: no studying while walking — feet stop, then eyes work — or kerbs and table corners collect their toll. And the sun rule holds here too: eyes never study the sun, whatever the game — hats and shade do the guarding on bright days.'},
'listen':{
 title:'Listen',one:'The doing-word for ears — a boy cupping a hand behind one ear, listening so hard the whole body helps.',
 seoTitle:'Listen Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: listen as the ears’ doing-word, the cupped-ear pose, whisper games, stillness for listening and the one-minute sound hunt.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the ears into detectives. Hear is the sense; listen is the decision to use it on purpose — and the card shows the trade’s oldest trick: a hand cupped behind an ear, gathering every sound in the room.',
 what:'Listen is what ears do on purpose: shut out the busy stuff and focus on one sound. The cupped hand behind the ear helps — it gathers sound like a little satellite dish. Listening is hearing with attention added, and it needs a still body to work best.',
 mean:'Hear is automatic; listen is chosen. The cupped hand makes the choice visible — a pose the whole world reads as paying attention. That pose is worth teaching: it turns waiting into an action, gives fidgety hands a job, and makes a three-year-old’s listening look as serious as it feels.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we listen with our ears, listening is hearing with attention — still body, cupped hands if wanted — listening works best when the body holds still, whispers are the best listening practice, and a good listener is a treasure at story time, music time and road-crossing time.',
 why:'Listening is the classroom skill hiding inside a game: stories, instructions, music and friendship all run on it. Naming listen gives the pose a word, the stillness a reason and the whisper games a purpose — and a child who can listen on request carries the skill straight into circle time.',
 dev:'Auditory attention and focus, the hear-listen distinction (automatic versus chosen), body-stillness and impulse control, whisper-voice play, and the cupped-ear pose as visible attention for group settings.',
 vocab:'listen, ear, cup, whisper, still, sound',
 teach:'1) Show the card and copy the pose: hand cupped behind the ear. What is the boy doing — listening hard! The cup makes the ears bigger and the listening official.\n2) Try the cup test: listen to the room with hands down, then hands cupped behind both ears. Louder? Different? The dish trick is real.\n3) Play the whisper game: send one whispered word across the room — cup the ear, lean in, catch it. Then swap: your child whispers, you listen.\n4) Prove the stillness rule: stomp once, then freeze — a listening body is a still body. Try listening while wiggling, then while frozen. Frozen wins every time.\n5) Go on a sound hunt: sit still for one minute, a finger up for each sound caught — clock, fridge, birds, a door somewhere. Compare catches at the end.',
 say:'"Listen! Your ears have a doing-word too: listen. Cup your hand behind your ear — see, it gathers the sound in! Listening works best when bodies hold still, so feet stop, hands cup, and we catch the sound: my whisper, the clock, a bird outside. What can your cupped ears find right now?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: where is the boy’s hand — cupped behind his ear? Which ear? Is he standing still? What word sits with the picture? Can you make the same listening pose?',
 ask:'"What do we listen with?" "What does the cupped hand do?" "Show me a listening body — what do the feet do?" "What did your cupped ears catch today?"',
 childSay:'Your child may adopt the cupped-ear pose for every story from here on, whisper everything for a day — secret word included — or freeze mid-game, hand to ear, and announce the post van two streets away before anyone else hears it.',
 real:'Listen is a daily instruction: listen to the kettle, listen for the timer, listen to grandma’s story, listen at the road. Each one works better on a child who owns the pose and the stillness — and the road one is the reason the practice matters.',
 play:'Sound catchers: everyone cups their ears and shuts their eyes for one minute, raising a finger for each sound caught, then compares lists — the fridge, the washing machine, a dog, a chair creak. Play it outdoors for bigger catches. Stillness with a mission is the whole trick.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we listen with? What does the cupped hand do? What do listening bodies hold?" The ears, the dish and the still answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Make the cupped-ear pose your signal at story time — hand up, ears cupped, room settles — because a pose works faster than a shout. And keep whispers precious: a voice that only whispers for secrets gets the best listening in the house.',
 safety:'Listening practice has a safety catch: the still-body rule matters most at the road — stop, look and listen is the crossing rhyme, and it works with a grown-up holding hands. Ears stay delicate too: no shouting into cupped ears, even for fun, and loud sounds get stepped away from.'},
'feel':{
 title:'Feel',one:'Feel — the skin’s doing-word: one careful hand reading something soft and white, fingertip by fingertip.',
 seoTitle:'Feel Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach the Five Senses (Class 21)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: feel as the skin’s doing-word, the three-texture corner, feelings and feels sharing one word and the check-with-a-grown-up rule.',
 intro:'This lesson sends the skin to work. Touch is the sense; feel is the doing-word — and the card keeps it gentle: one hand, moving slowly, reading something soft and white like a story written in texture.',
 what:'Feel is what skin does when it pays attention: a hand settles on something and reads it — soft, smooth, warm, squishy, bumpy. It is the doing-word for the touch sense, and the word wears two hats in your house: I feel the blanket and I feel happy share one useful verb.',
 mean:'Feel is the slow version of touch — not a grab, a read. The hand moves, the brain collects, the word arrives: soft. And the same verb carries feelings, which is a quiet piece of luck: a child who can say I feel the blanket is one step from saying I feel sad, and both sentences deserve the same listening.',
 know:'Your child needs to know: we feel with our skin, feeling means slow and careful — a hand that reads, not a hand that grabs — textures have names: soft, smooth, bumpy, warm, squishy — and feel talks about textures and feelings both: I feel the teddy, I feel happy.',
 why:'Feel is a two-for-one word: texture vocabulary and emotion vocabulary share it. Practising the texture side — blanket, sponge, spoon — builds the describing words preschool runs on, and the feeling side gives your child a ready-made way to name the inside weather: I feel happy, I feel cross, I feel cosy.',
 dev:'Tactile discrimination and texture vocabulary, the touch-feel verb link, emotional naming through the shared word feel, slow deliberate hand movement (reading, not grabbing), and comparison skills across textures.',
 vocab:'feel, hand, soft, smooth, squishy, cosy',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the hand doing — reading something soft with slow fingers? That careful reading is called feeling.\n2) Copy the hand: settle your palm on a cushion, shut your eyes, and read it — soft? squishy? warm? Fingertips first, slow as syrup.\n3) Build the texture corner: a blanket, one spoon and a sponge side by side. Feel each with eyes shut and say its word — soft, cold and smooth, squishy.\n4) Play the two-hats game: I feel the blanket — texture! I feel happy — feeling! Same word, two jobs. Make three sentences each and giggle at the crossover.\n5) Set the care rule: feeling hands go slow, and unknown things get a grown-up check first — radiators and bath water are for grown-ups to test, hands to confirm.',
 say:'"Feel! Your skin has a doing-word: feel. Put your hand down slow — like this — and read it: soft, smooth, warm, squishy. And feel has two jobs: I feel the soft blanket, and I feel happy — same word! Slow hands read best, and new things get a grown-up check first. What does this cushion feel like?"',
 notice:'Look at the picture together: what is happening — a hand reading something soft and white? Does the touch look slow or quick? What word sits with the picture? What did your fingers read today?',
 ask:'"What do we feel with?" "What did your hand feel today — soft? bumpy? warm?" "How does a feeling hand move — fast or slow?" "Say one sentence about a texture and one about a feeling!"',
 childSay:'Your child may run a fingertip along every wall and cushion in the house, filing reports — bumpy, smooth, cosy — declare I feel happy, I feel the teddy, I feel lunch, or test the two-hats rule on a grandparent for confirmation.',
 real:'Feel works everywhere: the warm bath tested with a toe, the soft jumper pulled close, the cold spoon at breakfast, the squashy sponge at sink time. Name what the hand read — your fingers felt it first — and the doing-word earns its keep twice a day.',
 play:'Texture lucky dip: a pillowcase hiding a wooden block, a scrunch of foil and a soft toy. One hand in, eyes shut: read the mystery with fingertips, guess, then pull it out to check. Swap the cast daily. Fingertip reading is the game; texture vocabulary is the prize.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we feel with? How does a feeling hand move? Name two textures you read today." The skin, the slowness and the words — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'Keep the texture corner to three objects and rotate weekly — three words done well beat ten rushed. And honour the second hat: when your child says I feel sad or I feel happy, treat it as the same skill practised — naming, exactly like the blanket got named.',
 safety:'Feeling hands get a check-first rule: radiators, bath water, oven doors and anything hot or unknown are tested by a grown-up first, then confirmed by careful fingers. Gentle is the touch — no squeezing pets, no prodding babies — and anything sharp or breakable stays on the look-only shelf.'}
};
